# Git policy

Git commands may run without a per-command approval prompt. This applies to standard git operations on this repo: status, diff, log, show, branch, add, commit, push to the existing remote, fetch, pull, checkout, switch, stash, remote, rev-parse. The founder chose this on 4 Nov 2026 to stop repeated prompts.

Limits:

- Never force-push (including `--force` and `--force-with-lease`), never `git reset --hard`, never `git clean`, never `git config` changes, never rewrite history on a shared branch, never create a new remote or a new hosted repo.
- Only non-git commands still need the usual approval. Auto-approval does not cover them.
- When the founder has not asked to commit or push in the current request, do not commit or push. Suggest the exact git command and ask them to confirm.
- When a git command is outside the commands this policy allows to run without a prompt, do not run it. Suggest the approval prompt they would need to give.
- Do not commit `node_modules`, `dist`, secrets, or `documents/_*.py` and `documents/_*.txt`.
